tonight I'm going to talk about the  cinematography on big hero six along  with the rendering in some of the  research we did so one of the early  mandates early in the development of the  film was as has been talked about  there's one this is going to be a huge  world it was gonna be this kind of  unseen scale we just haven't seen an  animated movie and also like Don was  talking about earlier it was going to be  the city San fransokyo which was kind of  this mashup of San Francisco and Tokyo  which is Michael said is something where  you know people really are familiar with  you know what those places look like and  what the light is is like and so we  wanted to be able to do really  believable light simulations for those  worlds as well and so what we did was we  developed our own render to help solve  this problem this started developing as  a big hero 6 was developing around the  same time and it solved both these  problems it could do physical lights and  simulations it was a global illumination  render that can do full ray tracing but  also like Kyle was talking about it  could handle kind of this unseen scale  which I'll talk a little bit about how  it did that what we call that is  streaming geometry and what that is is  in a ray tracer you have a camera here  and that camera fires out array it  actually fires a lot of rays out but if  I was out array and it hits something in  the scene and it samples samples that  and then that goes off and shoots array  and hits a light and and that's how we  we'd know that that rock has been  colored by that light and then we can  extend that and it it bounces more and  we call that one bounce and then you go  to this and you extend that and you can  see it gets really complicated quick in  fact you know generally a ray tracer out  there you need to have the whole  environment loaded at a single time with  Hyperion there's this idea of streaming  geometry where it bundles up rays that  are going in a similar direction and  then it figures out what geometry it's  going  hit it does a test for that and then it  only loads up that given geometry which  you can see represented here in the  color and what that lets us do is load  up much smaller chunks of the geometry  at a given time as opposed to having to  have everything loaded at at once and  then we could have much more complexity  and sophistication to any part of that  environment at one given time and that's  how we were able to achieve what Kyle  was talking about this kind of kind of  almost infinite amount of data we could  put into our cities at one given time  like I said we were developing this  really early on with big hero six it was  kind of a risky thing and so we are  encouraging all our artists to to grab  Hyperion I was getting develop and try  and test it see what we can do with it  give us feedback and how it's working  this is an image that one of our look  artists Alex Alvarado did the image on  the left is the self-portrait that he  did in Hyperion and the image on the  right is a character he did of his  supervisors we have fun dizzy so we can  go the next slide and so around that  same time this is this is about a year  you know Hyperion is really early on and  we wanted to kind of go really back to  kind of simplicity kind of like Zack was  talking about spheres with the the  animation we needed to do something  similar with the lighting so we we got a  bunch of spheres they're made up of a  different bunch of different materials  wood marble glass and and actually we've  got a ping-pong ball there's as well and  we have an lighting studio at Disney  Animation where we can we have a full  set of physical lights and cameras and  we can do tests and try different things  out and research so here we have the  photo reference that we took on the  screen left and then the Hyperion  recreation on the screen right you can  see how how close and and kind of  actually excited we were to see how well  things were working early on I should  mention here if you notice the ping-pong  ball on the screen left and the shadow  poking in and it has that really kind of  luminous fill  of the ping-pong ball lighting up that  shadow that wasn't actually what we got  our first tryout actually we got with  something more like this where you can  see you know I was talking about letting  the light rays bounce around once we get  to the lower screen left there where it  kind of looks like the ping-pong ball  looks like a kind of dirty kind of  plastic ball that's letting the buy  light bounce around one time and that's  typically what we were doing and a lot  of the industry does is you can really  only afford to let the light simulate  one bounce maybe two bounces what we  found is we needed to let D'Lite bounce  around ten times which was a little  scary because no one had had let things  go that much but we had to do it because  as you can see our main character baymax  here is made of on all much like that  ping-pong ball and as Donna was talking  about you know he needs to be really  appealing  he needs to be likable and as he  described huggable and as you see in the  screen left here bottom image he has a  dirty feeling if you just let the light  simulation go one bounce so we actually  had to let it go 10 bounces any shot  with baymax had 10 bounces of light  simulated and the environment here has  about 5 to 6 bounces you can actually  see that the difference not only in  baymax but the richness of the  environment when you let the light  bounce around and you get that warm  sunlight bouncing around it really added  to our imagery every shot in the movie  had at least five to six bounces of  light simulated and any shot with baymax  was 10
 

